  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An active weather pattern is likely from late \non Thanksgiving into next weekend. Forecast confidence on any \nparticular weather system is low, but travelers should continue \nto monitor this period for potential travel disruptions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nUpper low over the nrn Plains will be diving south into the MO \nValley today and At the same time a compact s/wv will be moving \nthru the srn Plains and induce cyclogenesis over FL this \nafternoon. This low will eventually get absorbed in the northern\nstream with the parent low pressure sitting up near the \nOH/PA/NY border by 12z Fri. \n\nGuidance is in agreement that precipitation will enter the area\novernight Thursday, and continue to move up into the area \nFriday morning. Regarding precipitation type, we have high \nconfidence in that freezing rain will occur Thursday night and \ninto Friday, with areas north of Bangor expected to have the \nhighest likelihood of freezing rain. Areas south of Bangor \nare more likely to be all plain rain with this system.\n\nWhere the low confidence lies with this system is how long it \nwill take surface temperatures to go above freezing, as the GFS\nand REFS wants to keep temperatures cooler longer then the \nEuropean and Canadian models. If cooler temperatures stay\nlonger, it could lead to longer period of freezing rain and\ngreater ice accretion. Air temperatures warm into the upper 30s\non Thursday, diminishing the threat for ice.\n\nAdditional chances for wintry mix/ snow showers are possible \nthrough the weekend, though confidence is low at this time. In \nsummary, it is still early, but be aware of the potential for \nactive weather that could impact travel Thanksgiving evening \nthrough the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dangerous marine conditions and possible \ncoastal impacts around the time of the midday high tide on \nFriday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nMinor coastal flooding is possible mainly around the midday Friday\nhigh tide with persistent southeast winds and enhanced swell\nheights. This could come as overwash/wave runup on more exposed\nsouth/east facing shorelines. Timing continues to favor the \nhighest storm surge near low tide on Friday morning, potentially\nlimiting the flood risk. We will continue to keep an eye on \ntiming, though, as this could influence the degree of coastal \nflooding.",
